## Decoding the question
The question has **three limbs**, and each needs its own headings. "**Trace the evolution**" asks for a dated history — from the first Women's Studies courses to Gender Studies — with the **reasons** for the change. "**Critically evaluate the status … in Pakistan**" asks for both **achievements and weaknesses**, ending in a judgement. "**Give your views**" on **autonomy versus integration** asks for an argued **position**, not just a summary of the two sides. Give each limb about a third of the answer.

## The model answer

### 1. Introduction

#### i) Definition
**Gender Studies** is the multidisciplinary study of how society turns biological sex into **gender** — the roles, identities and relations of **power** between women and men — and how gender intersects with class, religion, region and other identities. It grew out of **Women's Studies**, "the academic arm of the women's movement".

#### ii) The argument
The discipline evolved from a woman-centred field into the study of **gender relations**. In Pakistan it has become **established** — with centres, degrees and a place in the CSS scheme — but remains **fragile**, short of funds, local theory and influence over other subjects. The way forward is a **dual strategy**: a strong autonomous base that drives integration across the university.

### 2. The birth of Women's Studies

#### i) The first courses
Women's Studies was born in the late **1960s** in the United States, as second-wave feminists asked why universities taught history, economics and literature as if women did not exist. One of the first courses was taught at **Cornell** in ==1969==; the first full programme opened at **San Diego State College** in ==1970==; and the **National Women's Studies Association** was founded in ==1977==. In South Asia, India's report ***Towards Equality*** (==1974==) exposed women's declining status and sparked research.

#### ii) Its three aims

##### (a) Women as subject
To make women the **subject** of study, not a footnote to men's history.

##### (b) Women as knowers
To treat women as **knowers** whose experience counts as evidence.

##### (c) Research for women
To make research **benefit** women by informing law and policy.

### 3. Why Women's Studies became Gender Studies

#### i) The concept of gender
**Ann Oakley** (*Sex, Gender and Society*, ==1972==) separated biological sex from social gender; **Gayle Rubin** (==1975==) described the "**sex/gender system**"; and **Joan Scott** (==1986==) called gender "a **primary way of signifying relationships of power**".

#### ii) New subjects
**R. W. Connell**'s *Gender and Power* (==1987==) founded the study of **masculinities**, and **queer theory** (around ==1990==) questioned fixed categories of sex and sexuality.

#### iii) Internal critique
**bell hooks** (==1981==) and **Chandra Talpade Mohanty** (==1984==) argued that "woman" in Women's Studies often meant only white, Western, middle-class women.

#### iv) The development agencies
Donors and governments moved from **WID to GAD** — from "women" to "gender relations" — and asked universities for gender expertise.

::: fig timeline | From Women's Studies to Gender Studies — and to Pakistan.
@layout vertical
1969: Cornell — one of the first Women's Studies courses
1970: San Diego State — the first full programme
1972: Oakley separates sex from gender
1975: Shirkat Gah founded in Karachi
1977: National Women's Studies Association (US)
1983: Bowles and Klein frame autonomy versus integration
1986: Scott — gender as a relation of power
1987: Connell — masculinities enter the field
1989: Five Women's Studies centres in Pakistan's universities
2016: Gender Studies becomes a CSS optional subject
:::

### 4. What changed, and what stayed

#### i) What changed
The shift changed the **subject** — from women alone to **gender relations** among women, men and gender-diverse people — and the **key idea**, from women's oppression to gender as a **social construction** and a relation of power. It added men's studies, sexualities and **intersectionality**.

#### ii) What stayed
The feminist commitment to **equality**, the critique of male-centred knowledge, and the use of many disciplines.

#### iii) The risk, and the resolution
Critics such as **Tania Modleski** (*Feminism Without Women*, ==1991==) warned that "gender" could make **women invisible again**. The best reading is that the two are **partners in one project**, which is why many departments now call themselves "Women's and Gender Studies".

### 5. The Pakistani roots

#### i) Research outside the universities
In Pakistan, gender scholarship began **outside the universities**. **Shirkat Gah** (==1975==) and the **Aurat Foundation** (==1986==) researched women's rights, work and law.

#### ii) The movement as scholar
The **Women's Action Forum** (==1981==) challenged the **Hudood Ordinances** of ==1979==, producing legal and historical research. **Khawar Mumtaz and Farida Shaheed**'s *Women of Pakistan: Two Steps Forward, One Step Back?* (==1987==) wrote the first history of the movement.

#### iii) Why this matters
The academic field grew from **activism** — a point that answers the charge that it is a foreign import.

### 6. Institutions: the status today

#### i) The 1989 centres
In ==1989== the **Ministry of Women's Development** set up **Women's Studies centres** in five public universities — **Quaid-i-Azam** (Islamabad), **Karachi**, **Punjab** (Lahore), **Peshawar** and **Balochistan** (Quetta). The centre at Quaid-i-Azam University became the **Centre of Excellence in Gender Studies**.

#### ii) Degrees and journals
Most centres were later renamed Gender Studies and now offer degrees up to the **PhD**. Journals such as the *Pakistan Journal of Gender Studies* and *Alam-e-Niswan* publish local research.

#### iii) Official recognition
Gender Studies became a **CSS optional subject** from **CE-2016**.

### 7. Achievements

#### i) Evidence for the laws
The discipline's research on violence, law, work and health supplied the case for the **workplace harassment law** (==2010==), the **acid** and **anti-women practices** laws (==2011==), the **honour-killing** amendment (==2016==) and the domestic-violence laws from Sindh (==2013==) to Islamabad (==2025==).

#### ii) People for the state
Its graduates staff the **National Commission on the Status of Women** (==2000==) and provincial commissions, and it informed the **National Policy for Development and Empowerment of Women** (==2002==) and the **Gender Reform Action Plan**.

#### iii) Words for hidden harms
It gave the public **words** for hidden harms — harassment, economic abuse, the double burden.

### 8. Problems: a critical evaluation

#### i) Financial
Much research depends on **short donor projects**, so priorities follow funding rather than national needs.

#### ii) Intellectual
Teaching relies heavily on **Western texts in English**; local theory, Urdu and regional-language material and reliable data are thin. There are too few trained teachers, and libraries outside the big cities are poor.

#### iii) Political
The field faces **resistance** and the charge of a "**Western**" or "**anti-family**" agenda. It is often **ghettoised** — a small, mostly female department with little influence over law, medicine or economics.

#### iv) Weak impact on outcomes
The country's outcomes remain poor: Pakistan ranked ==148th of 148== in the Global Gender Gap Report ==2025== and ==143rd of 145== in ==2026==.

### 9. The debate: autonomy and its risk

#### i) The source
The classic statement of the debate is **Gloria Bowles and Renate Duelli Klein**'s *Theories of Women's Studies* (==1983==).

#### ii) What autonomy offers
**Autonomy** means a **separate department** with its own teachers, courses, degrees, budget and research — "a house of its own". It offers depth (from first degree to PhD), room for new theory, an independent critical voice and visibility.

#### iii) Its risk: ghettoisation
The field becomes a "women's corner", ignored by the rest of the university, costly to maintain and, in poorer countries, dependent on donors.

### 10. Integration and its risk

#### i) What integration offers
**Integration** (or mainstreaming) means carrying gender into **every subject** — gender in medicine's maternal health, in law's family and inheritance law, in economics' unpaid care, in planning's safe transport. It reaches every student, changes the disciplines themselves and involves men.

#### ii) Its risk: dilution
Gender is reduced to a single token lecture — "**add a lecture and stir**" — dependent on a few sympathetic teachers, with expertise and the feminist edge lost.

::: fig compare | Autonomy and integration — each needs the other.
@vs +
## Autonomy
- Depth, theory, degrees, a critical voice
- Risk: ghettoisation
## Integration
- Reach into every subject and student
- Risk: dilution
:::

### 11. My view: the dual strategy, made local

#### i) Both, not either
The debate is best resolved by a **dual strategy**: a strong autonomous centre that trains specialists **and** drives gender into other departments by training their teachers and co-teaching their courses. This mirrors the **Beijing Platform**'s (==1995==) "**twin track**" of gender mainstreaming plus special measures.

#### ii) For Pakistan
Pakistan built **autonomy first** (1989) but **integration** remains weak.

##### (a) Integrate the core subjects
Gender modules in **medicine, law, economics, education and civil-service training**.

##### (b) Fund and localise
Steady **HEC** funding instead of donor cycles, and **local research** in Urdu and regional languages.

##### (c) Link to the state
Partnerships with the NCSW, the Bureau of Statistics and the Women's Parliamentary Caucus. Integration grounded in Pakistani data is also the best answer to the "Western agenda" charge.

### 12. Conclusion

#### i) The evolution in a sentence
Women's Studies became Gender Studies as the idea of gender, the study of men and masculinities, queer theory and intersectionality widened its gaze from women to the relations of power between the sexes.

#### ii) The verdict
In Pakistan the discipline, rooted in the women's movement and institutionalised in ==1989==, has **arrived** — it has degrees, research, influence on the laws of 2010–2025 and a CSS paper. But it is **not yet secure**. A dual strategy — autonomy as the **engine**, integration as the **road** — offers the surest way to move it from the margins to the mainstream.

## Why this answer scores
- Answers **all three limbs** under separate headings: the evolution, the status and a clear **view**.
- The evolution is **dated** (1969, 1970, 1972, 1977, 1986, 1987) and explains **why** the shift happened.
- The status is **evaluated**, not described: achievements are matched by financial, intellectual and political problems.
- The view is **argued** — the dual strategy, adapted to Pakistan — and answers the "Western agenda" charge.
- A **timeline** and a **compare** figure show the argument at a glance.
